						const body = "\nTo anyone who has ever tried to lose weight, build muscle, or simply understand their body composition, the world of calorie tracking can feel incredibly confusing. You hear advice like “eat less, move more” or “reduce your carbs,” but these suggestions ignore the underlying biological equation of energy balance. \n\nIf you want to achieve predictable, science-backed changes in your physique, there is one metric you must understand above all others: your **TDEE**, or **Total Daily Energy Expenditure**.\n\nIn this guide, we will break down exactly what TDEE is, explore the four physiological components that dictate how many calories you burn each day, examine the science behind calculation formulas, and explain how to apply this number to achieve your physical goals.\n\n---\n\n## What is TDEE?\n\n**Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE)** is the total number of calories your body burns in a 24-hour period. It represents the sum of all energy required to keep your body alive, digest the food you consume, perform daily tasks, and engage in physical exercise.\n\nFrom the standpoint of physics, the human body is a thermodynamic system. It takes in energy in the form of chemical bonds (food calories) and expends energy to maintain order, perform mechanical work, and release heat. \n\n*   If your calorie intake matches your TDEE, you are in **caloric balance** and your weight will remain stable.\n*   If your calorie intake is below your TDEE, you are in a **caloric deficit** and your body will burn stored tissues (fat and muscle) for energy, resulting in weight loss.\n*   If your calorie intake exceeds your TDEE, you are in a **caloric surplus** and your body will store the excess energy, resulting in weight gain.\n\n---\n\n## The Four Components of TDEE\n\nYour TDEE is not a single, monolithic number. Instead, it is the product of four distinct metabolic processes:\n\n### 1. Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR) — 60% to 75% of TDEE\nYour **Basal Metabolic Rate** is the energy expended by the body to maintain basic life-sustaining cellular functions while at complete rest in a temperature-neutral environment, after fasting for 12 hours. Even if you lay completely still in bed all day without moving a muscle, your body still requires a massive amount of energy to power your brain, beat your heart, filter blood through your kidneys, and maintain cellular ion gradients. For most people, BMR is the largest component of TDEE.\n\n### 2. Non-Exercise Activity Thermogenesis (NEAT) — 15% to 30% of TDEE\n**NEAT** is the energy expended for everything we do that is not sleeping, eating, or sports-like exercise. It includes walking to your car, standing at a desk, washing dishes, typing on a keyboard, and even unconscious actions like fidgeting or maintaining posture. \n\nNEAT is the most variable component of TDEE between different people. A construction worker or postal carrier will have a much higher NEAT (burning up to 1,000–1,500 extra calories per day) than an office worker who sits at a desk for eight hours. Research shows that NEAT also drops unconsciously when you enter a calorie deficit, as the body tries to conserve energy.\n\n### 3. Thermic Effect of Food (TEF) — ~10% of TDEE\nDigesting, absorbing, transporting, and storing nutrients requires energy. When you eat food, your body must expend calories to break down the macronutrients. This is known as the **Thermic Effect of Food**. \n\nTEF varies depending on the macronutrient composition of your diet:\n*   **Protein** has the highest thermic effect, requiring 20% to 30% of its energy content to be digested.\n*   **Carbohydrates** require 5% to 15% of their energy content.\n*   **Fats** require the least energy, using only 0% to 3% of their energy content.\n\nTherefore, eating a higher protein diet naturally increases your TEF, slightly boosting your TDEE.\n\n### 4. Exercise Activity Thermogenesis (EAT) — 5% to 10% of TDEE\n**EAT** is the energy expended during structured, deliberate exercise, such as lifting weights, running, cycling, or playing sports. Although many people focus heavily on workouts for burning calories, EAT is actually the smallest contributor to TDEE for the average individual. A hard 45-minute gym session may only burn 300 to 500 calories, which is easily offset by sitting for the rest of the day.\n\n---\n\n## How is TDEE Calculated?\n\nTo find your TDEE without living in a metabolic laboratory chamber, we use a two-step mathematical estimation:\n\n### Step 1: Calculate BMR\nWe use the **Mifflin-St Jeor equation**, which is considered the gold-standard formula for adults:\n*   **Men:** $BMR = (10 \\times \\text{weight in kg}) + (6.25 \\times \\text{height in cm}) - (5 \\times \\text{age in years}) + 5$\n*   **Women:** $BMR = (10 \\times \\text{weight in kg}) + (6.25 \\times \\text{height in cm}) - (5 \\times \\text{age in years}) - 161$\n\n### Step 2: Apply Activity Multiplier\nOnce BMR is established, we multiply it by a physical activity factor based on the Harris-Benedict multipliers:\n*   **Sedentary** (little or no exercise): $BMR \\times 1.2$\n*   **Lightly Active** (exercise 1–3 days/week): $BMR \\times 1.375$\n*   **Moderately Active** (exercise 3–5 days/week): $BMR \\times 1.55$\n*   **Very Active** (hard exercise 6–7 days/week): $BMR \\times 1.725$\n*   **Extra Active** (very physical job + daily exercise): $BMR \\times 1.9$\n\n### Worked Example:\nLet's look at a 30-year-old female who weighs 65 kg (143 lbs), stands 165 cm (5'5\") tall, and exercises moderately 3–5 days per week.\n1.  **BMR Calculation:**\n    $$BMR = (10 \\times 65) + (6.25 \\times 165) - (5 \\times 30) - 161$$\n    $$BMR = 650 + 1031.25 - 150 - 161 = 1,370.25 \\text{ kcal/day}$$\n2.  **TDEE Calculation:**\n    $$TDEE = 1,370.25 \\times 1.55 \\approx 2,124 \\text{ kcal/day}$$\n\nHer maintenance target is **2,124 calories per day**.\n\n---\n\n## How to Use TDEE to Modify Body Composition\n\nOnce you have determined your daily calorie burn (TDEE), you can adjust your caloric intake to target weight loss, maintenance, or muscle gain:\n\n### 1. For Fat Loss (Caloric Deficit)\nTo lose fat, you must consume fewer calories than your TDEE. A safe, sustainable deficit is **15% to 20% below your TDEE** (typically subtracting 300 to 500 calories).\n*   For the female in our example: $2,124 - 500 = 1,624 \\text{ calories/day}$.\n*   This will yield a fat loss rate of approximately 0.5 kg (1 lb) per week.\n\n### 2. For Muscle Gain (Caloric Surplus)\nTo build muscle efficiently while minimizing fat gain, you should consume a small surplus of **5% to 10% above your TDEE** (typically adding 200 to 300 calories) alongside consistent strength training.\n*   For our example: $2,124 + 250 = 2,374 \\text{ calories/day}$.\n\n### 3. For Maintenance (Weight Stability)\nTo maintain your current weight, eat at your TDEE. This is also useful for \"diet breaks\" to help recover metabolic rate after an extended weight loss period.\n\n---\n\n## Why Knowing Your TDEE Beats Calorie Guessing\n\nGeneric guidelines, such as the standard \"2,000 calories a day\" label on nutrition facts, are designed as broad population averages. They do not account for your height, age, current weight, muscle mass, or daily steps. Relying on these generic baselines can result in eating too much (and failing to lose weight) or eating too little (causing extreme hunger, muscle loss, and metabolic adaptations).\n\nBy calculating your individual TDEE, you establish a personalized baseline. This allows you to track weight trends and adjust your calories systematically based on your body's actual responses.\n\n---\n\n## Summary of Key Takeaways\n*   **TDEE** is the total energy you burn in 24 hours.\n*   It is composed of your **BMR** (60–75%), **NEAT** (15–30%), **TEF** (10%), and **EAT** (5–10%).\n*   Your TDEE shifts naturally as your weight and daily activity change.\n*   Recalculate your TDEE every 4 to 6 weeks to avoid weight loss plateaus.\n\n---\n\n## 🧮 Try the TDEE Calculator\n\nReady to find your personal TDEE and set accurate targets for fat loss or muscle gain? Use our free, 100% private tool to calculate your numbers instantly:\n\n👉 [TDEE Calculator](file:///calculator/tdee)\n";
